SUMMARY:[現場] Daniel Wurgaft | A Resource Rational Analysis of Represent
 ations: Geometry\, Inference\, Capacity
LOCATION:Stanford Neurosciences Building\, James Lin and Nisa Leung Seminar
  Room\, E153
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds-seminar-daniel-wurgaft
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds-seminar-daniel-wurgaft\n
 \nAbstract\n\nNeural systems process information by forming representation
 s of the world. Studies of representational geometry have revealed remarka
 bly structured representations in biological and artificial systems\, as w
 ell as surprising alignment in representations across systems. Why do repr
 esentations across systems converge to similar structures\, how do these s
 tructures encode concepts and uncertainty over them\, and how are they sha
 ped by resource constraints?\n\nHere\, we tackle these questions in neural
  networks through the lens of resource rationality\, asking what represent
 ations are optimal for solving a task\, given dimensionality and linear re
 adout constraints. We derive a notion of optimal linear representations\, 
 and show that these emerge across varied architectures and tasks\, providi
 ng a simple explanation for representational convergence as alignment towa
 rd the optimal solution. This shared treatment motivates a normative forma
 lization of conceptual spaces that generalizes the linear representation h
 ypothesis. We then extend this account to uncertainty and inference over c
 onceptual spaces\, asking how representations should change in response to
  in-context evidence\, and finally to resource-constrained settings involv
 ing representational interference.\n\n\nAbout the Center for Neural Data S
 cience Seminar Series \n\nThe Center for Neural Data Science Seminar Serie
 s is a platform for trainees across campus to share  insights and innovati
